# Parse module specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the implemented mm.parse module: the shared shell
scanner both shell profiles drive, and the number and time readers other
modules delegate to. docs/modules-shell.mdy owns the shell grammars and what
each profile does with a token; docs/modules-json.mdy owns what JSON accepts
as a number; this document owns what mm.parse reads and reports.

mm.parse has no use: edge. Its interfaces need only the standard library's
cstddef, cstdint, and string_view; its number reader also uses charconv,
cmath, and a std::string for the digits it hands to strtod. It is core, and
bootstrap.sh and tools/build/main.cpp compile it before mm.json and
mm.configure, which depend on it.

# Partitions

- mm.parse:dialect declares Dialect, the TokenKind superset, FragmentKind,
  SourceSpan, the Sink seam, and the shared character predicates.
- mm.parse:cursor declares ScanOutcome and Cursor.
- mm.parse:number declares NumberKind, NumberValue, parse_number_at, and
  parse_number.
- mm.parse:time declares DateTime, Duration, TimeKind, TimeValue,
  parse_time_at, and parse_time.
- mm.parse re-exports all four.

# Dialects and tokens

Dialect selects one of two shell lexical rules. Embedded is mm.shell's level
1: a line ends at \n, at \r\n, or at a bare \r, only space and tab are
blank, and a word is reported as a sequence of fragments. Full is
mm.shell.full's level 3: a line ends at \n, \r is blank, and no fragments are
produced.

TokenKind is the union of both dialects' token kinds: End, Newline, and Word;
the embedded operators AndIf, OrIf, Semicolon, DoubleSemicolon, LeftParen,
RightParen, LeftBrace, RightBrace, Bang, and CaseBar; and the full operators
Pipe, Input, Output, Append, HereDocument, DuplicateInput, DuplicateOutput,
and IoNumber. mm.shell's TokenKind is an alias of this one; mm.shell.full
translates it to its own.

FragmentKind names the parts of an embedded word: Literal, SingleQuoted,
DoubleQuoted, Escaped, Parameter, Arithmetic, and CommandSubstitution. A
fragment inside double quotes is marked quoted.

SourceSpan is a byte offset and length into the text being scanned. It is the
span type of both shell profiles too: mm.shell's SourceSpan is an alias of it.

# The cursor

Cursor(text, at, dialect) scans text from byte offset at. It owns nothing and
allocates nothing. scan(sink) skips blanks, comments, and line continuations,
then reads one token and returns a ScanOutcome:

- complete is false when the token cannot be read; message then names why;
- at_end is true at the end of the text, with kind End;
- kind and span are the token and its bytes;
- next_offset is where the following scan starts.

The cursor reports a token through sink.emit_token and each embedded fragment
through sink.emit_fragment, passing sink.context back unchanged. It never
reports Newline through the sink: a dialect that needs newline tokens, as the
full profile does, emits them itself from the outcome. A caller that consumes
text on its own, as the full profile does for a here-document body, starts a
new Cursor at the offset after it.

Refusals carry these messages, which the shell profiles map to their own
statuses:

- "unclosed quote", "unclosed parameter expansion", "unclosed command or
  arithmetic substitution", and "trailing escape" are incomplete: more text
  could finish them;
- "backtick substitution is not supported", "here-document variant is not
  supported", "asynchronous execution is not supported", and "excluded shell
  construct" are unsupported;
- "NUL in script" and a refusal without a message are malformed.

The embedded dialect refuses NUL anywhere, a comment included, and refuses
&, <, >, and a backtick outside a word as operators it does not have. The full
dialect reads an IoNumber as a word of digits alone ended by < or >, and
refuses [[, ]], ((, <(, and >(.

In the embedded dialect every expansion is a fragment, inside double quotes
as well as outside, and a backslash-newline inside double quotes is a line
continuation that ends the current fragment. A parameter name starts with a
letter of either case or an underscore.

# Numbers

parse_number_at(text, at) reads one number starting at at, after any leading
space, tab, \r, or \n. parse_number(text) is parse_number_at(text, 0). Neither
requires the number to end the text: length and consumed say how much was
read, and a caller that needs the whole text to be a number compares them.

A number has an optional sign, then either a radix prefix 0x, 0o, or 0b with
digits of that radix, or decimal digits with an optional fraction and an
optional exponent. An underscore may appear among the digits and is ignored. A
trailing point without fraction digits is accepted; an exponent without
digits is not.

NumberValue reports:

- kind: Invalid, Integer (a prefixed radix, or decimal digits alone), Float
  (a fraction), or Scientific (an exponent);
- integer, for an Integer: the value as a signed 64-bit number. A decimal
  magnitude is read unsigned, so -9223372036854775808 is in range;
- real: for a Float or Scientific, the nearest double; for a decimal Integer,
  its nearest double too, so a reader wanting a real number of any width takes
  it;
- overflow: for a decimal Integer beyond the signed 64-bit range, integer is
  clamped to that range's end; for a Float or Scientific out of double's
  range, real is signed zero on underflow, subnormal results included, and
  signed infinity on overflow.

A caller keeps its own grammar on top: mm.shell's arithmetic accepts decimal
digits alone and checks them before asking mm.parse for the value, and
mm.json refuses a number whose real is infinite.

# Times

parse_time_at(text, at) and parse_time(text) read one time value after any
leading blanks, trying in order:

- a Duration: decimal digits followed by exactly one of s, m, h, d, or w and
  nothing else;
- an Epoch: ten to twelve decimal digits followed by the end or a blank, also
  converted to a DateTime in UTC unless it overflows;
- a Time: HH:MM or HH:MM:SS, with hours below 24 and minutes and seconds
  below 60;
- a Date: YYYY-MM-DD or YYYY/MM/DD, optionally followed by T or a space and a
  time, which makes it a DateTime; or MM/DD/YYYY. The day must exist in that
  month of that year, leap years included.

TimeValue reports kind, the span read, the date or duration or epoch, and
overflow. DateTime mirrors mm.rtc's so a module that does not import mm.rtc
can still read dates; weekday is zero, unspecified, for anything parsed.

# Consumers

- mm.shell's embedded scanner drives the cursor with the Embedded dialect and
  counts fragments through its own writer.
- mm.shell.full's scanner drives it with the Full dialect, emits Newline
  tokens, and handles here-document bodies itself.
- mm.shell's arithmetic, mm.shell.full's redirections, mm.sketch's String
  conversions, mm.configure's compiler version digits, and mm.json's integer
  and number all read numbers through parse_number.
- mm.json's extract_time and extract_number expose parse_time and
  parse_number to JSON callers.

# Conformance

- tests/mm/parse pins both dialects' cursors, the sink callbacks and error
  recovery, every number form including radix prefixes, underscores, and
  edge values, and every time form including leap days and invalid dates.
- tests/mm/shell and tests/mm/shell/full pin what each profile builds on the
  cursor, and tests/mm/json pins JSON's number policy over parse_number.
